+\section{Post-review: Oracle Allocations of a Different Shape}
+\label{app:shapeoracle}
+
+CORACLE re-aims C10's quota multiset by true endpoint error and so tests direction at a fixed shape. To test shape, we pre-registered three oracle allocations at $N = 1000$, built from the same endpoint-error vector and served by the same selector: \emph{OR-PROP}, quotas proportional to each cluster's error; \emph{OR-TOP10}, $100$ images to each of the ten highest-error clusters; and \emph{OR-TOP5}, $200$ to each of the five highest. \emph{OR-TOP10-SHUF} gives OR-TOP10's multiset to clusters drawn by a frozen permutation. All use endpoint labels to choose training data, so COD10K-test is not an unbiased endpoint for them; NC4K labels are read by no allocation. B, C10 and CORACLE are the committed runs, reused after a reuse gate (identical code path, data bytes and selections; committed metrics reproduced at six decimals; identical first training step).
+
+Two facts were fixed before any training. Because per-cluster error spans only $0.09$--$0.47$, the error-proportional quota is nearly flat ($4$--$24$ per cluster); every permutation of it leaves at least $84\%$ of the selected images unchanged, so its direction cannot be tested with this selector and its shuffle was not trained. And concentration does not determine effective rank: a perfectly even quota under the same matching is still separable from a random draw (held-out $d = 0.5191$) with a lower participation ratio (ratio $0.855$), while the five-cluster shuffle keeps a near-random ratio ($0.9433$).
